// BUILD / FLASH (needs the TinyUSB USB stack for the Settings -> "USB drive" mode; sketch.yaml sets it):
//   arduino-cli compile --build-path /tmp/pgbuild .
//     (same as --fqbn adafruit:samd:adafruit_pygamer_m4:usbstack=tinyusb)
//   arduino-cli upload  -p <PyGamer port> --fqbn adafruit:samd:adafruit_pygamer_m4:usbstack=tinyusb --input-dir /tmp/pgbuild .
//   Libraries: Adafruit Arcada, Adafruit TinyUSB Library, Adafruit SPIFlash, SdFat - Adafruit Fork.
//
// PyGamerPortal
//
// A first-person portal puzzle game for the Adafruit PyGamer, rendered
// with a classic grid raycaster (Wolfenstein 3D style: one ray per screen
// column, walls as distance-scaled vertical strips). Floor-level features
// that a raycaster can't show as walls (portals, the goal, keys, boxes,
// lasers) are drawn as camera-facing billboard sprites instead.
//
// Note: portals here teleport on touch but don't render a see-through
// view into the linked location -- true portal rendering needs real
// perspective/stencil 3D, which is well beyond both a raycaster and this
// microcontroller.
//
// Mechanics:
//   - Fire a portal onto the wall you're facing (A = blue, B = orange).
//     Walk into either linked portal and you come out the other one.
//   - Pits ('chasms') block walking but not your aim -- shoot across one
//     to plant a portal on the far side.
//   - Boxes can be pushed. Push one into a pit and it fills the pit,
//     becoming permanent floor. Push one into a portal and it teleports
//     out the linked portal. Push one in front of a laser emitter and it
//     blocks the beam.
//   - Pressure plates open every door in the level for as long as
//     something (you, or a box) is standing on one. Leave a box parked on
//     a plate to hold a door open while you walk on through.
//   - Ice slides you until you hit something solid -- aim before you step
//     on.
//   - Some walls are portal-proof -- shooting at one fails outright, so
//     you need to find a different angle.
//   - Laser emitters fire a deadly beam in a fixed direction. Walking into
//     an unblocked beam resets the level. Block it with a wall, a closed
//     door, or a box.
//
// Movement is first-person: up/down walk forward/backward along the
// direction you're facing, left/right turn that direction, with proper
// wall-sliding collision. Portals always fire wherever you're currently
// looking (snapped to the nearest tile-aligned direction).
//
// Controls:
//   Joystick Up/Down   - walk forward/backward
//   Joystick Left/Right- turn
//   A                  - fire a BLUE portal
//   B                  - fire an ORANGE portal
//   Start (tap)        - reset the current level
//   Start (hold ~0.5s) - toggle 3rd person
//   Start (hold ~1.5s) - level-select / difficulty menu
//   (any button)       - advance to next level once you've won
//
// Select ("menu" button) is the quick menu key everywhere; the Start/D-pad
// alternatives below still work too:
//   - In any game: tap Select to open the pause menu (Start-hold also works).
//     Block Stack is the exception: Select rotates there (like A).
//   - Pause menu / Settings / Challenges hub / Trophies / Credits / High Scores / level
//     pick menu / game title screens: Select = back (same as B).
//   - Game picker: Select = Settings now (B tap / B-hold unchanged).
//   - Level editor: Select = Save/Exit menu. Portal: Select = level menu
//     (title, in play, after a win) and reroll maze inside the level menu.
//
// Not yet hardware-validated: this whole rendering approach (raycaster
// wall projection, sprite billboarding/occlusion, FOV, colors, sound and
// NeoPixel timing) is a first pass and will need tuning once seen on the
// real screen.

#include <Adafruit_Arcada.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>   // sbrk() for freeRamNow()

